\section{Three Contracts Behind Declarative Rewriting}

A compiler can describe an operation's \emph{shape}, a rewrite's \emph{meaning}, and a legalized implementation's \emph{cost} independently. Fehr, Niu, Riddle, Amini, Su, and Grosser's \textit{IRDL: An IR Definition Language for SSA Compilers} makes structural constraints declarative, including constraints that require multiple operands or results to have the same type. Such a schema can help verify a replacement built by a rewrite, but it does not show that the replacement has the same semantics. Even an operation which takes two equal-width inputs and returns an equal-width value needs a separate specification for overflow, exceptional behavior, and effects.

Graph rewriting can have a quite different role. In \textit{LEAN: An Intermediate Language Based on Graph Rewriting}, Barendregt and colleagues use rules to describe computation itself. Graphs can encode sharing and cycles, so reducing a shared node is not simply reducing two independent occurrences of the printed term. An optimizer operating on a graph IR has a related implementation concern: before removing a node after a match, it must account for all users, not just the one followed by the matcher. But it also must distinguish the program's evaluation rules from the compiler's transformations, which must preserve the allowed behavior of the program as a whole.

\section{Worked Example: Covering an Address Expression}

Take the pure word expression \texttt{add(base,shl(index,2))}, defined with wrapping arithmetic on a given width \(w>2\). Suppose machine A can compute this expression in one scaled-address instruction with matching width and wrap semantics. Machine B has separate shift and add instructions. A declarative selector might declare the following patterns and illustrative costs, with \texttt{reg} standing for an available value in the required register class:

\begin{verbatim}
reg <- add(reg, shl(reg, 2))  cost 1  [A, matching width]
reg <- add(reg, reg)          cost 1
reg <- shl(reg, 2)            cost 1
\end{verbatim}

With zero cost for the two available input registers, the specialized cover costs one on A and the composed cover costs two on B. Fraser, Henry, and Proebsting's \textit{BURG: Fast Optimal Instruction Selection and Tree Parsing} builds a tree matcher from a cost-augmented grammar. The benefit of such a matcher is that a parent rule can be compared with the combination of cheaper-looking child rules, under the tree cost model. It does not prove an optimal schedule or register allocation for the whole graph. If another operation also uses the shifted value, selection might still need a separate shift, and the cost of a shared graph cannot in general be obtained by summing separate tree covers.

The width predicate is part of legality, not profitability. Using a wider operation with no truncation can produce a different result at the boundary: at \(w=32\), with \(\texttt{base}=0\) and \(\texttt{index}=2^{30}\), the specified result is zero, not \(2^{32}\). If the source operator instead checked overflow, wrapping at the selected width would also be wrong. A rule test should exercise those boundary cases, and a rule validator can prove a bit-vector identity for the admitted width and separately check the target feature and operand classes at match time.

\section{Retarget Without Leaking Target Choices}

Lewis, Deutsch, and Goldstein's \textit{Clarity MCode: A Retargetable Intermediate Representation for Compilation} illustrates a split between portable type information and target-specific restrictions. Primitive sizes are explicit in MCode, while the backend determines platform alignment and aggregate layout from type information. Its native interface has ABI obligations even though much of the IR is machine independent. Likewise, in a rule system, legality checks must include ABI, register, and addressing constraints whenever generated code exposes them. Changing rule priorities should affect cost, not weaken those checks.

An oriented simplifier needs a terminating strategy or an explicit budget; including both directions of an identity in a greedy rewrite loop can make the compiler cycle. The dossier's Markdown Chapter~10, \textit{Equality Saturation Compiler}, explores keeping multiple equal forms instead of prematurely choosing one, and Chapter~29, \textit{Proof-Carrying IR and Certified Rewrite Contracts}, considers evidence for rule use. Whether alternatives are collected or chosen immediately, check operation schemas, semantic side conditions, and target legality separately. This keeps the addition of a new backend from silently redefining a common operation.
